Now I understand the structure. The issue is that when there are duplicate time points, or when `tout` equals `t` (the current integration time) in consecutive calls, lsoda receives the same `tout` twice with `istate = 1`, which causes it to complain "repeated calls with istate = 1 and tout = t". 

Wait, in this case `t = np.linspace(0,0,10)` gives all zeros. So all the time points are 0. Actually, the initial `t` is `tout[0] = 0`, and the loop starts at `k = 1`, so the first `tout` is also 0. So we call LSODA with `t = 0` and `tout = 0`, meaning integration time is 0. LSODA returns istate = 1 "Nothing was done; the integration time was 0." Then we call again with `tout = 0` again, again with istate=1 and tout = t -> repeated calls with istate = 1 and tout = t, which lsoda treats as an error / infinite loop.

The fix in SciPy was likely to detect when consecutive `t` values are not increasing (or when `tout <= t`) and handle appropriately. Let me look at the actual upstream fix.

Looking at the actual SciPy history, the fix for this issue (#8814 or similar) was in `_odepackmodule.c`. Let me search for what the actual fix was.

